<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="27d2bee2-065b-489b-ac2e-e3b9106b5325" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerTroubleshootingDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      		<para>
      					DemiCode Scheduler executables are protected to prevent theft of intellectual property.
                        Debugging these executables using Win32 debuggers will therefore be hard or impossible.
                        Extensive logging capabilities have therefore been built into Scheduler to enable
                        error condition diagnostics.
			</para>
			<para>
						<legacyBold>Note:</legacyBold> In case you receive exceptions that originate from the DemiCode.Scheduling
                        assembly the stack traces from these exceptions should be reported to <externalLink>
						<linkText>support@demicode.com</linkText>
						<linkUri>mailto:support@demicode.com</linkUri>
						</externalLink> for further analysis of the error condition.
			</para>
    </introduction>
                                    
 
<section>
  <title>Diagnostics using logging</title>
  <content>
    <para>
      DemiCode Scheduler comes with support for logging using the log4net framework. This
      framework enables simple yet powerfull logging capabilities.
      Internally, the Scheduler logs both exceptions and verbose debug information during schedule and task execution.
      The same framework is exposed to third-party tasks.
    </para>
  </content>
  <sections>
    <section>
      <title>Enable logging using code</title>
      <content>
        The Scheduling system uses the notion of a logger factory to provide access to logger frameworks. To enable logging in a scheduler we must pass a logger factory 
        implementation instance to <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Scheduling.SchedulerFactory</codeEntityReference> when calling Create.
        The following sample shows how to do this with the built-in <codeEntityReference qualifyHint="false">T:DemiCode.Logging.log4net.Log4netLoggerFactory</codeEntityReference>.
      <code language="c#">
        ILoggerFactory loggerFactory = new Log4netLoggerFactory();
        using (Scheduler scheduler = SchedulerFactory.Create(loggerFactory))
        {
          // ... start scheduler processing
        }
      </code>
      </content>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Enable logging using configuration</title>
      <content>
        <para>
                        Default configuration
                        for applications like the Scheduler Service uses an EventLog appender to log errors
                        and warnings to the Windows Event Log. Other appenders, like FileAppender and AdoNetAppender,
                        can easily be configured to enable logging to files and databases.
			</para>
			<para>
                        The following configuration sample can be used to diagnose problems in a Scheduler-powered application. 
                        Errors and warnings will be logged to the Windows Event log. All other information is logged
                        to a rolling log file. The "root level" with value "ALL" ensures that all information
                        levels will be passed through to the appenders.
			</para>

			<code language="xml">
&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;utf-16&quot; ?&gt;
&lt;configuration&gt;
	&lt;configSections&gt;
		&lt;section name=&quot;scheduling&quot; type=&quot;DemiCode.Scheduling.Configuration.SchedulerSettings, DemiCode.Scheduling&quot;/&gt;
		&lt;section name=&quot;log4net&quot; type=&quot;log4net.Config.Log4NetConfigurationSectionHandler, log4net&quot; /&gt;
	&lt;/configSections&gt;

	&lt;scheduling defaultScheduler=&quot;prod&quot;&gt;
		&lt;schedulers&gt;
			&lt;scheduler name=&quot;prod&quot;&gt;
			    ...
				&lt;loggers&gt;
					&lt;Log4netLogger /&gt;
				&lt;/loggers&gt;
				...
			&lt;/scheduler&gt;
		&lt;/schedulers&gt;
	&lt;/scheduling&gt;

	&lt;log4net&gt;

		&lt;root&gt;
			&lt;level value=&quot;ALL&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;appender-ref ref=&quot;EventLog&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;appender-ref ref=&quot;FileLog&quot; /&gt;
		&lt;/root&gt;

		&lt;appender name=&quot;FileLog&quot; type=&quot;log4net.Appender.RollingFileAppender, log4net&quot; &gt;
			&lt;file value=&quot;.\\Schedulerlog.txt&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;appendToFile value=&quot;true&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;lockingModel type=&quot;log4net.Appender.FileAppender+MinimalLock&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;rollingStyle value=&quot;Date&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;datePattern value=&quot;yyyyMMdd-HHmm&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;layout type=&quot;log4net.Layout.PatternLayout&quot;&gt;
				&lt;conversionPattern value=&quot;%date [%thread] %-5level %logger [%ndc] - %message%newline&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;/layout&gt;
		&lt;/appender&gt;

		&lt;appender name=&quot;EventLog&quot; type=&quot;log4net.Appender.EventLogAppender, log4net&quot; &gt;
			&lt;filter type=&quot;log4net.Filter.LevelRangeFilter&quot;&gt;
				&lt;levelMin value=&quot;WARN&quot; /&gt;
				&lt;levelMax value=&quot;FATAL&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;/filter&gt;
			&lt;applicationName value=&quot;DemiCode Scheduler Service&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;layout type=&quot;log4net.Layout.PatternLayout&quot;&gt;
				&lt;conversionPattern value=&quot;%date [%thread] %-5level %logger [%property{NDC}] - %message%newline&quot; /&gt;
			&lt;/layout&gt;
		&lt;/appender&gt;
	&lt;/log4net&gt;
&lt;/configuration>                
			</code>
      </content>
    </section>
    
  </sections>
</section>
   
            

<section>
  <title>See Also</title>
  <content>
  	<para>Logging from your own task in <link xlink:href="252dfa96-85bf-4da0-917e-6f837a2b746d">Implementing ITask</link>.</para>
  	<para>For more information on configuring loggers and appenders go to the <token>log4netWeb</token>.</para>
  </content>
</section>
           
            
            
      
    

